---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import RoleMeta from "../../components/RoleMeta.astro";
import { site, links } from "../../config";
import {
  openRoles,
  rolePath,
  applyHref,
  isExternalApply,
  jobPostingJsonLd,
} from "../../careers";

// Roles are cards and everything else on the page is a hairline stack, the same
// weighting the research index uses for published work against unwritten work.
// The difference between "there is a job here" and "there is a company here"
// should not be something you have to read to see.

// How the team works, which is the part a good candidate is actually deciding
// about. Kept to four, and each one is a thing that is already true rather than
// a value we would like to have.
const how = [
  {
    title: "Open by default",
    body: "The CLI, the board it was built on and this website are all public repositories. Working in the open is the default here and closing something is the decision that needs a reason.",
  },
  {
    title: "Evidence before claims",
    body: "Everything this site says about the agent is either measured or marked as unmeasured. The research section exists so the numbers can be recomputed by someone who has no reason to believe us.",
  },
  {
    title: "Small, and slow to grow",
    body: "Every hire is a large fraction of the team, so the process is short and the answer comes back quickly either way. You talk to the people you would work with, because there is nobody else to talk to.",
  },
  {
    title: "Hardware in the loop",
    body: "We work together in one room in Bengaluru, next to the boards. Benches, parts and fab runs are paid for. Nobody has ever verified a layout by reading it.",
  },
];

// An ItemList of role URLs, not the postings themselves. Embedding the full
// JobPosting nodes here published every posting at two addresses, this page and
// the role page, which is duplicate-posting territory and bought the listing
// nothing: a list page is not a shape Google reads postings out of. The list
// carries only roles that are genuinely posted, since jobPostingJsonLd returns
// null for a draft and a draft must not reach a job index. See the note on
// Role.posted in src/careers.ts.
const posted = openRoles.filter((r) => jobPostingJsonLd(r));

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CollectionPage",
  name: "Careers at copperhead",
  url: new URL("/careers/", site).href,
  ...(posted.length
    ? {
        mainEntity: {
          "@type": "ItemList",
          itemListElement: posted.map((r, i) => ({
            "@type": "ListItem",
            position: i + 1,
            url: new URL(rolePath(r.slug), site).href,
          })),
        },
      }
    : {}),
};
---

<Base
  title="Careers · copperhead"
  description="Open roles at Chouhan Industries, the team building copperhead: an open source agent that designs circuit boards and verifies its own work against KiCad."
  path="/careers/"
  ogImageAlt="Careers at copperhead"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Navbar home={false} />
  <main id="top">
    <article>
      <div class="wrap">
        <header>
          <p class="kind">Careers</p>
          <h1>Work on copperhead</h1>
          <p class="standfirst">
            We are a small team building an agent that designs circuit boards
            and then proves its own work. The tool is open source, the board it
            was proven on is open hardware and the benchmark behind both is
            public, so most of what you would build here is something you can
            show to whoever hires you next.
          </p>
        </header>

        {
          openRoles.length > 0 ? (
            <>
              <h2 class="head">Open roles</h2>
              <ul class="roles">
                {openRoles.map((r) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={rolePath(r.slug)}>
                      <RoleMeta
                        accent
                        items={[
                          r.discipline,
                          r.location,
                          r.workplace,
                          r.commitment,
                        ]}
                      />
                      <h3>{r.title}</h3>
                      <p>{r.summary}</p>
                      <span class="more">Read the role</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}

                {/* The open application, as the last row rather than as a role.
                    It is deliberately not an entry in `roles`: it has no
                    responsibilities, nothing to verify and no date, and a
                    fabricated Role would sit one `posted` field away from
                    emitting JobPosting markup for a job that does not exist.
                    Marked out from the real listings by a dashed rule and no
                    copper tag, so it reads as the end of the list rather than
                    as a fourth opening. */}
                <li class="open-application">
                  <a
                    href={applyHref()}
                    {...(isExternalApply()
                      ? { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" }
                      : {})}
                  >
                    <RoleMeta
                      items={["Open application", "Bengaluru, India", "On-site"]}
                    />
                    <h3>Something we have not written down</h3>
                    <p>
                      The three above are not the whole list of things this
                      needs. If you would be good here and none of them
                      describes you, say what you do and why it belongs near
                      this problem.
                    </p>
                    <span class="more">Introduce yourself</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </>
          ) : (
            /* Not an error state. A team this size is closed to applications
               most of the time, and saying so plainly is better than a page
               that implies a pipeline it does not have. */
            <p class="none">
              There are no open roles right now. The section below is still the
              honest description of the work, and the last paragraph on this
              page still applies.
            </p>
          )
        }

        <h2 class="head">How we work</h2>

        <ul class="how">
          {
            how.map((h) => (
              <li>
                <h3>{h.title}</h3>
                <p>{h.body}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        <h2 class="head">If none of that fits</h2>

        <p class="open">
          Roles here get written around a person about as often as the other
          way round. If you have built something in this area and nothing above
          describes it, send the thing itself: a board, a repository, a
          teardown, a bug report you are still pleased with. It tells us more
          than a CV does and it is what we would ask for on the second email
          anyway.
        </p>

        <p class="apply">
          {/* The global .btn/.btn-primary pair, not a local button: this is the
              same action as the navbar CTA and should not be a second opinion
              about what a primary button looks like. */}
          <a
            class="btn btn-primary"
            href={applyHref()}
            {...(isExternalApply()
              ? { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" }
              : {})}
          >
            Introduce yourself
          </a>
        </p>

        <p class="meanwhile">
          The <a href={links.repo}>repository</a> is the fastest way to work out
          whether you would enjoy this, and the <a href={links.blog}>blog</a> is
          the argument behind it. Both are open to be picked apart before you
          write to us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  article {
    padding-block: 150px 120px;
  }

  header {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .kind {
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--copper);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
  }

  .standfirst {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  /* Every second-level heading on the page is the same tracked mono label, so
     the three groups read as peers rather than as a hierarchy. */
  .head {
    margin: 4.5rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  /* The first one follows the standfirst rather than another block, and wants
     the wider gap the research index puts above its card list. */
  header + .head {
    margin-top: 4rem;
  }

  .roles {
    list-style: none;
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--hairline);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .roles li a {
    display: block;
    background: var(--bg);
    padding: 2.25rem 0;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }

  .roles li a:hover {
    background: var(--surface);
  }


  .roles h3 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 26ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .roles p {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    max-width: 64ch;
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.97rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .more {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--copper);
  }

  .roles li a:hover .more {
    color: var(--copper-hi);
  }

  /* Not an opening, so it is tinted off the page rather than sitting on it like
     the real listings, and its label carries no copper. A dashed rule was the
     first attempt and it does not work here: the separators are the grid's 1px
     gap showing the list's own background through, so a border would have drawn
     a second line directly under the first.

     Selected through .roles so it outweighs `.roles li a`, which sets the
     background these rules are overriding. */
  .roles .open-application a {
    background: var(--panel);
  }

  .roles .open-application a:hover {
    background: var(--panel-hi);
  }

  .open-application h3 {
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .open-application a:hover h3 {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .none {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  /* Hairline stack, not cards: this is context around the roles and should not
     compete with them. Same treatment as the research index's unwritten list. */
  .how {
    list-style: none;
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .how li {
    padding: 26px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .how h3 {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .how p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .open {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    max-width: 64ch;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .apply {
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
  }

  .meanwhile {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  @media (min-width: 721px) {
    .roles li a {
      padding-inline: 24px;
      margin-inline: -24px;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    article {
      padding-block: 120px 80px;
    }

    .standfirst {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    .roles li a {
      padding-block: 1.85rem;
    }

    .how li {
      padding: 22px 0;
    }
  }
</style>
